PROJECT SETUP & CONTEXT — GenomicGapID
1) Project Overview

This project focuses on:

analyzing large bacterial genome FASTA files
mapping sequences from terminal_windows.tsv to genomes
identifying primer locations (forward + reverse)
extracting amplicons
building a discrimination-based scoring system for primer selection

Key workflow principle:

Structure and scoring logic come before full-scale data processing
2) Core Biological Rules (CRITICAL)
FASTA Files
FASTA files contain forward strand only (5’ → 3’)
All sequence comparisons must respect strand orientation
Sequence Orientation

Each sequence has an orientation:

Positive (+)
→ use sequence as-is
Negative (-)
→ must compute reverse complement before matching
Reverse Primer Logic
Reverse primer binds to forward strand
Must use inverse complement before searching
Sequence Types
terminal_windows.tsv:
contains genomic sequences (~400 bp)
NOT actual primers
Primers:
~14–15 bp subsequences within those regions
Amplicon Expectations
Amplicon length should match:
genome location
values in gene_amplicon_summary
3) Algorithm & Workflow Direction
Priority Order
Define scoring system
Build data structures
Extract biological data
Run optimization
Discrimination Scoring (Planned)
Score per primer pair
NOT across pairs in a set

Steps:

Generate genome profiles per primer pair
Perform pairwise comparisons
Assign score (0–2)
Normalize (0–1)
Average across genomes
Important Constraint
Same-length amplicons are indistinguishable

Example:

two genomes → both produce 200 bp
result → single signal
must penalize in scoring
Goal
Identify primer sets with:
high discrimination
clear score gap between best and second-best
4) VS Code Project Structure
GenomicGapID/
├── data/
│   ├── raw_fastas/        # large FASTA files (NOT tracked in Git)
│   ├── references/        # metadata, TSV files
│   └── processed/         # outputs (ignored)
│
├── scripts/               # main Python code
│   ├── fasta_utils.py
│   ├── primer_utils.py
│   ├── scoring.py
│   └── pipeline.py
│
├── notebooks/             # exploratory work
│   └── original_notebook.ipynb
│
├── results/               # outputs (ignored)
├── docs/                  # documentation
│   └── project_setup_and_context.md
│
├── .gitignore
├── README.md
└── requirements.txt
5) Environment Setup
Python virtual environment: .venv
Selected in VS Code (bottom-right)
All packages installed inside .venv

Key packages:

biopython
pandas
jupyter
6) Git & Data Rules
Tracked in Git:
scripts
notebooks (lightweight)
README
documentation
NOT tracked:
data/raw_fastas/
data/processed/
large outputs

Reason:

FASTA files are large
Git is not designed for large datasets
7) Code Design Principles
General
modular code (no monolithic scripts)
reusable functions
clear separation of logic
File Roles
fasta_utils.py
reading FASTA files
sequence searching
indexing
primer_utils.py
orientation handling
reverse complement logic
scoring.py
discrimination scoring
penalty handling
pipeline.py
orchestration of workflow
8) Development Workflow
Step-by-step
Test logic on small FASTA files
Validate correctness
Scale to full datasets
Optimize performance if needed
Migration Strategy
move code from notebook → scripts gradually
test each component before continuing
9) Codex Usage Guidelines

Codex should be used for:

writing functions
debugging code
structuring modules

Codex should NOT be relied on for:

biological correctness
algorithm design decisions
Prompting Strategy

Always include:

orientation rules
FASTA assumptions
sequence type (genomic vs primer)

Example:

FASTA is forward strand only. If sequence orientation is '-', reverse complement before searching.

10) Immediate Next Steps
Build FASTA search function:
input: sequence + orientation
output: start/end indices
Validate on test FASTA
Expand to:
multiple genomes
forward + reverse primer pairing
Begin amplicon extraction
11) Long-Term Goal

Develop a pipeline that:

maps primer pairs to genomes
extracts amplicons
evaluates discrimination ability
selects optimal primer sets
12) Key Principle

Build structure first, then scale.